# Roulette mapping: always produce the overlay PNG

Whenever you add, move, or re-measure a roulette click target (hitbox area, click
center, UI button, bet cell), you **must** regenerate the visual overlay and show it
in your reply. Never report a mapping change with coordinates alone.

## Run after every mapping change

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_map_overlay --ip 10.0.0.90
```

Use `--input <screenshot.png>` when the cabinet is busy or unreachable, and
`--mode ui|board|stats|all` to limit the view.

`stats` maps the modal statistics screen, so it needs a capture taken while that
panel is open:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_map_overlay --input _tmp_logs/map_overlay/verify_topleft/stats_closed.png --mode stats
```

## Outputs (always overwritten)

- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_all.png` — bets + UI
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_ui.png` — UI chrome only
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_board.png` — bet cloth only
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_stats.png` — statistics modal
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_verified.png` — live pass/fail per bet spot
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_anchors.png` — inside-bet anchors from the registry
- `_tmp_logs/map_overlay/screen_base.png` — raw capture used

## Two skins, one toolchain

Every tool takes `--layout layout1|layout2` and the cabinet must already be showing
that skin, because the scripts click real pixels.

| | layout1 `futura_doublezero` | layout2 `futura_doublezeroCrycle` |
|---|---|---|
| dozens | above the numbers | **below** the numbers |
| spin | countdown disc, top centre | `START` button, bottom right |
| opacity | top centre, next to the disc | under `CRÉDITO`, top left |
| skin selector | `LAYOUT_SWITCH` (cards+dice, top right) | `PANO` (top right) |
| square ↔ race | `CHANGE_VIEW`, bottom right of cloth | `CAMBIAR VISTA`, right rail |
| history strip | chevrons at both ends | plain capsule, no chevrons |
| extras | `LOCK`, `LLAMAR`, `CALIENTE_FRIO` | `MENU`, `SERIES` / `SERIES EXTRA` rails |
| statistics | hold the history strip | tap the `JUGADOR` badge (`PLAYER`) |

Geometry lives in one module per skin — `automation/roulette_ui_areas.py` (layout1)
and `automation/roulette_layout2_areas.py` (layout2) — each registering a
`Surface` in `automation/roulette_surface.py`. Never reuse one skin's boxes for
the other; nothing transfers except the 12-column grid shape.

layout1 keeps the paths above; other skins render into a subfolder, e.g.
`_tmp_logs/map_overlay/layout2/overlay_all.png`. Rebuild a registry with:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_layout2_full_map
```

## Sub-screens are part of the map

A skin is not mapped when the cloth is mapped. Controls that only exist after a
click live in that skin's `UI_OVERLAYS`, keyed by screen, and `Surface` folds
them into the same registry with an `overlay` field. layout2 has five, and none
of the panels close with Escape:

| screen | opened by | closed by |
|---|---|---|
| `statistics` | `PLAYER` (JUGADOR badge) | `STATS_EXIT` (SALIR) |
| `language` | `IDIOMA` | `LANG_ES` / `LANG_EN` (no cancel) |
| `help` | `AYUDA` | `HELP_EXIT` (SALIR) |
| `menu` | `MENU` | a second tap on `MENU` |
| `racetrack` | `CHANGE_VIEW` (window open) | a second tap on `CHANGE_VIEW` |

Render one with a capture of it open:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_map_overlay --layout layout2 --input shot.png --mode screen:help
```

The racetrack is a *view*, not a modal, and it carries two betting surfaces:

- the oval's 38 pockets, `race_<number>`, found by masking the band, taking the
  enclosed non-white patches as pockets, filling the numerals with
  `binary_fill_holes`, then inscribing the largest square in each — the pockets
  are slanted quads, so their bounding boxes overlap while the inscribed squares
  do not. Numbers come from wheel order pinned to the two green pockets
  (`_tmp_logs/layout2/measure_race.py`).
- a complete small cloth inside the oval, `mini_<id>` for every id the big cloth
  has — 38 numbers, dozens, evens, `2 a 1` (`_tmp_logs/layout2/measure_mini.py`).
  Its `2 a 1` column has no drawn right border; the fill ends one column width
  past the grid.

**Read the help book before guessing.** `AYUDA` opens `INFORMACIÓN DEL JUEGO`,
whose legend names every control in the game's own words — that is where
`MUESTRA GANANCIAS` turned out to be a max-win toggle rather than a panel, and
where `DENOMINACIÓN` and `STANDARD 36X` turned out to be choosers that this
cabinet has nothing to populate (both are inert on `.90`, in or out of a betting
window).

## Re-map the screen that is open, when the software team moves something

The **Map current screen** button on the Roulette tab (and
`automation/roulette_screen_map.py`) maps whatever the cabinet is showing rather
than a fixed target list. Use it when a drop moves controls or adds a screen.

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_screen_map --ip 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2 --list
python -m automation.roulette_screen_map --ip 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2 --detect
python -m automation.roulette_screen_map --ip 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2 --screen help --apply
python -m automation.roulette_screen_map --ip 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2 --new-screen "history v2" --apply
```

It captures the screen (twice, until two frames agree — panels arrive a second or two
after the click and a single shot photographs the screen you just left), names the
screen it matches, then re-finds each mapped box by searching for its baseline crop
nearby. Verdicts per control: `unchanged`, `moved` (registry updated), `covered`
(something is drawn over it, box kept), `lost`, `featureless` (not drawn right now).
Verification is hybrid — cloth screens go through the three-role middleware session,
panels through `roulette_verify_ui` probes.

Baselines live next to the overlay captures (`screen_<key>.png`, with `base` and
`race` keeping their existing names) and `--apply` refreshes them — except a cloth
baseline while a panel is open over it, which would bake the panel into the cloth. A
baseline that depicts something else loses every control at once, and the tool says so
instead of rewriting geometry. After any `--apply` that moved boxes, regenerate the
overlay as below.

What the live runs on `.90` taught it, each of which had first gone wrong:

- **Check the skin before the screen.** Layout coordinates clicked on the other skin
  land on whatever *that* skin draws there; a layout2 sweep on a layout1 cloth pressed
  the skin selector and swapped the cabinet mid-session, then saved the wrong baseline.
  The number grid's own borders identify the skin with no baseline at all (2.3 for the
  skin that is up, 0.6 for the other), and the tool refuses on a clear mismatch. With a
  full-screen panel up there is no cloth to measure, so it says so and carries on.
- **Judge identity on edges, not pixels.** "No más apuestas" dims everything: the cloth
  measured 0.64 *changed* against its own baseline, worse than a capture of another
  skin. Correlated gradients ignore the dim; the same rescaling is applied before
  relocation, or every control reads as having moved.
- **Ask each screen separately whether it is up.** More than one is: the menu rail is
  drawn over the cloth, and the language chooser leaves the cloth matching at 0.17.
  Panels are scored inside their own footprint (0.00 open, 0.84 closed), and only a
  *bare* cloth (≤0.10) rules a panel out.
- **Refuse to move a control on a mediocre match.** Real controls match at 100%; the
  look-alikes the search settles for behind a rail scored 71-85% and proposed five bets
  that had not moved.
- **Discovered controls are hints.** They are drawn on `candidates.png` in the run
  folder for review and stay out of the registry unless `--accept-candidates` is
  passed, because a lit neighbour pill looks exactly like a new button — and a written
  box is a click target for the bot.

## Prove what the chrome does, don't assume it from the icon

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_verify_ui --layout layout2
python -m automation.roulette_verify_ui --layout layout2 --group toggle,chip
python -m automation.roulette_verify_ui --layout layout2 --write-hitboxes
```

Each probe clicks the control, screenshots the cabinet and diffs it. Three
things the sweep learned the hard way, which its `Plan` entries now encode:

- **Effects arrive late.** The view swap, the rails and the chips repaint a
  second or two after the click, so a control that looks inert gets a second
  look before it is written off.
- **A hidden control is not an inert one.** The `SERIES` / `SERIES EXTRA` pills
  are drawn only while their rail is toggled on, and the bare cloth behind them
  swallows clicks. `Plan.needs_visible` turns the rail on first. Visibility is
  read from *edge density*, never brightness: the pills dim during "no más
  apuestas", so a brightness test reports the betting phase instead.
- **Both eye glyphs on a rail are the same toggle.** They are drawn as an open
  eye and a crossed eye, but tapping either flips the pills, and tapping one of
  them twice turns them on and then off. Hence `SERIES_EYE_TOP` /
  `SERIES_EYE_BOTTOM` rather than `SHOW` / `HIDE`.

Two controls are mapped from the help book and never pressed: `MENU_PIN_LOCK`
locks the cabinet behind an unknown PIN, and `MENU_ASSISTANT` raises a service
call that no click of ours can take back.

## Prove bet spots against the middleware, don't eyeball them

A box in the right place can still be the wrong bet. Verify with real clicks and
`PlayerDataBets` as ground truth, then render the verdict map:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --ip 10.0.0.90            # all 50 mapped spots
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --ip 10.0.0.90 --only "0,00,17"
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --write-hitboxes           # fold proof into layout1
python -m automation.roulette_map_overlay --ip 10.0.0.90 --mode verify
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2           # same sweep, Crycle skin
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --write-hitboxes
```

`--mode all` also writes `overlay_verified.png` whenever that skin already has a
`results.json`, so one render call produces the whole set.

Quote `--only` in PowerShell: an unquoted `0,00,17` is parsed as an integer array
and `00` silently collapses to `0`.

Inside bets (splits, streets, corners, six-lines, basket) are swept the same way
with `--anchors`, which walks the `anchor_unverified` / `anchor` entries and writes
`results_anchors.json` so the 50-spot verdict map stays untouched:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --anchors
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --anchors --write-hitboxes
```

The racetrack view is the third group: `--family race` for the oval's pockets and
`--family mini` for the small cloth inside it. Either puts the cabinet on the oval
first (`ensure_view` presses `CAMBIAR VISTA` inside an open window and proves the
swap against `screen_race.png`) and writes `results_race.json`:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --family race,mini --batch 1
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --family race --write-hitboxes
```

To re-check one slice of the cloth, name families instead of spelling out ids:
`--family straight|outside|split|street|corner|sixline|basket|trio|race|mini`
(comma-separated, and the inside-bet families imply `--anchors`). Each group keeps
its own results file, so a run may not mix them.

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_verify_bets --layout layout2 --family split,corner
```

Every spot the sweep does not touch keeps its previous verdict, so the written
summary still covers the whole skin.

A proven anchor loses its `anchor_unverified` kind. This is also how mapping
mistakes surface: an anchor that drifts off the shared edge reports a straight
instead, and the reply names the bet the core really has there. That is how the
zero column got fixed on layout2 — cell 2 straddles the 0/00 divider, so its whole
edge is **one** three-number `Split 0+2+37` (there is no `0+2` or `2+00`), and the
five-number `Basket 0+1+2+3+37` lives on the **outer bottom corner** of the zero
column, not at the divider. Probe an unknown pixel with
`_tmp_logs/layout2/probe_points.py 244,684`.

Cabinet facts the verifier depends on (measured on `.90`):

- Betting is open **22 s** out of a **40 s** cycle; a bet still standing when the
  window shuts is played for real and the stake is lost. `CancelAllBets` inside
  the window refunds it, so the sweep costs nothing.
- Straights report `BetType=Fields`, `Id` = the number, with **`00` → `Id=37`**.
- Outside bets (dozens, columns, even-money) are **dropped silently** below the
  outside minimum of ~10 credits — they look identical to a missed click, so the
  verifier skips them and says so instead of reporting a false failure. Top up
  with `.\Invoke-WinDivertAftRoulette.ps1 -ComputerName 10.0.0.90 -NonRestricted
  -AmountCents 500000 -Send` (≈6 min; `-DryRun` first) before sweeping them.

## Prove a box by its corners, not its middle

A hitbox that is a few pixels too big still passes a centre-click test. The corner
probe clicks **1 px inside** the top-left and bottom-right of every mapped box and
requires both clicks to produce the *same* event — bets via `PlayerDataBets`,
chrome via the `Sending put action` lines in `godot1`, with a screenshot diff as
the fallback for controls that only redraw.

**Do not tail the logs to check whether a bet click landed.** On `.90` the client
logs `Sending put action` only for `MenuCommands`, `SetChip`, `Paytable`,
`SetNeighboursPower` and `AdminMenu`; `PlaceBet` and `CancelAllBets` appear in no
log under `var\log` at all, and our own HTTP PUTs bypass the client so they are not
logged either. A watcher over a live bet sweep therefore reports "no betting
activity" while bets are visibly booking — poll `/api/data` instead. The window
markers (`open` / `closing` / `closed`) *are* in `godot1`, which is why `BetWindow`
reads them from there, and note those lines are **UTC** while `ruleta` logs local.

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_edge_probe --target 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2
python -m automation.roulette_edge_probe --target 10.0.0.90 --layout layout2 --family straight
python -m automation.roulette_edge_probe --target local --layout layout2 --only "17,20" --batch 1
python -m automation.roulette_edge_probe --layout layout2 --list   # plan only, no clicks
```

Verdicts: `same` (sound), `differs` (the box spans a border), `top_left` /
`bottom_right` (only that corner registered — the box is too big), `silent`,
`skipped` (box too thin for two distinct corners). Nothing is ever auto-applied;
the pack lands in `_tmp_logs/edge_probe/<layout>_<target>/` as
`EDGE-TEST-<layout>.md` + `edge_results.json`.

What it costs and how it is kept cheap:

- Number spots share a read — one script clicks eight corners, one
  `PlayerDataBets` read names every bet that landed — and neighbours are kept out
  of the same batch so a click that slips one cell lands on something no other
  spot in the batch claims. A batch whose bet count does not match its identified
  clicks is re-probed singly instead of reported on a guess.
- **The sweep refuses to start with nothing to judge by.** Bets need the middleware
  answering; chrome without `--pixel` needs the godot log folder. This is not
  paranoia: `C:\Goldclub` on a cabinet is a junction to `G:\`, so swapping the
  mounted install turns a roulette machine into a slot machine while SMB keeps
  answering — which once produced two sweeps and 165 meaningless verdicts. If a run
  reports every control `silent`, check the product before the mapping.
- **`race_*` and `mini_*` are their own pass**, with `CHANGE_VIEW` pressed first and
  the view proved by capture. Clicked on the square cloth they book whatever is under
  those pixels, which is how `race_0` once "booked" `Dozen:13-24`. If the view will
  not switch they are `skipped`, never guessed at. The square cloth is restored after.
- `PANO` and `LAYOUT_SWITCH` are in `NEVER_CLICK`: a skin swap mid-sweep invalidates
  every control after it, because the registry being swept describes one skin.
- Three units failing the same way in a row stops the sweep — a pack of identical
  infrastructure errors is not a report — and the untouched controls say so.
- Chrome is judged from the log alone by default. The screenshot fallback that
  separates "this button only redraws" from "the click missed" costs three cabinet
  captures a control (~45 s each), and since the client logs only five action
  names, most of the chrome lands in it — a full sweep with it on runs for hours.
  Ask for it explicitly with `--pixel`, ideally with `--family chrome --only ...`.
- The chip buttons are probed **last**: `chip_100` arms a 100-credit stake, and a
  bet probed after it is played for a hundred times the intended price. The chip is
  also re-armed to 0 before every bet unit, and a sweep stops probing bets below
  `CREDIT_FLOOR` base units rather than reporting refused bets as missed clicks.
- Verdicts are checkpointed to `edge_results.partial.json` after every unit, so a
  dropped share does not cost the whole run. It is deleted once the pack is written.
- The betting phase is *tracked* rather than waited for (`BetWindow`), so several
  probes share one 22 s window. Every cloth bet is cancelled inside the window it
  was placed in, so a sweep costs nothing.
- **Named bets are excluded by default.** EVEN, ROJO, 1-12, `2 a 1` and their
  racetrack twins are dropped silently below the outside minimum, which is
  indistinguishable from a missed click. They are recognised by the *number count*
  in their registry `expect.Id` (a chip bet covers at most six), not by where they
  are drawn — that is what keeps `mini_ODD` out while `mini_17` stays in. Ask for
  them by name or with `--family outside` after topping the credits up.

**The cloth reserves a band along every cell border for the split that shares it.**
Clicking 1 px inside `17`'s mapped box books `Split:15+18` at the top-left and
`Split:17+20` at the bottom-right, because the registry boxes trace the drawn
cells and the game hit-tests the borders as inside bets. So a straight failing the
corner test is not automatically a mapping error, and the probe measures the band
rather than guessing: for each failing number spot it walks both corners inwards
(3, 6, 10, 16, 24 px) until the read shows the number and nothing wider, then
writes that inset as the suggested box. Inside-bet anchors are never refined — they
are *meant* to sit on a shared border. Skip the measurement with `--no-margin`.

## The GUI's "Map current screen" button

Same engine, driven from Automation → **Map current screen**, and it works in the
shipped exe without a rebuild:

- **Target**: a cabinet over the admin share, or **this computer** — the roulette
  window if it is running here, otherwise the whole primary monitor.
- **Connection pre-flight** runs before the screen list is usable and blocks OK
  when something required is missing: SMB 445, the `C$` admin share and WinRM for a
  cabinet; the input agent and a screen locally. Each failure carries the command
  that fixes it (`cmdkey /add:<ip> ...`, `Enable-WinRM.ps1`). Optional parts —
  logs, `:8090`, whether the game is running locally — only warn.
- **Map picture**: every run writes `map_review.png` into its pack
  (`automation_runs/<stamp>_<target>_screenmap_<layout>_<screen>/`) with every
  mapped box drawn on the capture it was mapped from, plus a banner and a legend
  (kept / moved / new / covered). The **Show map picture** button opens the newest
  one. Report a mapping change with that image, never coordinates alone.
- **Edge test** checkbox runs the corner probe straight after the mapping run.

Hitbox and calibration JSON are read from and written to a **writable** folder —
`automation\layouts` next to `LogInvestigator.exe`, seeded from the bundle on first
use, the repo folder in a dev run. Reading them from the PyInstaller bundle would
lose every mapping change when the exe exits. Run packs go to `automation_runs`
beside the exe, never the current directory.

## Embed it in the reply

Use forward slashes or the chat will not render the image:

```markdown
![overlay](C:/Users/Ezbogar/GoldclubLogInvestigator/_tmp_logs/map_overlay/overlay_all.png)
```

Backslash paths (`C:\Users\...`) do **not** render.

## Review the render, don't just ship it

Read the PNG back and check boxes land on the real controls. If one is off,
adjust that skin's areas module, rebuild its registry, re-render, and look again.
Measure from detected cloth separator lines / button borders rather than guessing
percentages — `_tmp_logs/layout2/measure_*.py` shows the approach (colour masks →
row/column runs → box bounds).

`tests/test_roulette_layout2.py` guards the result: it fails if any two boxes in a
skin overlap, if a click point falls outside its own box, or if an inside-bet
anchor uses an id the `PlaceBet` catalog does not know.

Pick outline colours that survive the underlying pixels — `0`/`00` were invisible
while drawn green on the green pockets.

## Sliders need their travel drawn, not just a box

A box around a slider says nothing about where a tap lands. Mapped sliders live in
each skin's `UI_SLIDERS_PX` (`handle_min_x`, `handle_max_x`, `track_y`) and the
overlay draws the travel line with `0.0` / `1.0` end ticks plus a panel inset.

Calibrate the ends by tapping a ladder of known x positions and reading the handle
back, not by eyeballing the track fill: taps inside the travel park the ring at
`tap - 0.5 px`, and taps beyond it clamp, which is what pins the real ends. On
layout2 the drawn track suggested 404..519 while the control actually clamps at
397.5..519.5, so `--value 0.0` was 6 px short until it was measured. Set or read
one with:

```powershell
python -m automation.roulette_slider --ip 10.0.0.90 --read
python -m automation.roulette_slider --ip 10.0.0.90 --value 0.5
python -m automation.roulette_slider --layout layout2 --value 1.0
```

Each capture-and-read round trip costs ~45 s, so probe a whole ladder in one
script run rather than one value per command.

Always leave OPACIDAD back at `1.0` when finished — a mid-track value changes how
the GUI renders and skews later pixel-diff heuristics.
